<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nafud</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Great Nafud, An Nafud</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Nafud is a large sand desert in the north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-peninsula">Arabian Peninsula</a>, in northern Saudi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>. Covering some 65,000 square kilometres, it is known for its high red dunes and harsh climate. It lay across routes linking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hejaz">Hejaz</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, and travellers such as Lady Anne Blunt described crossing it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nafud.jpg" alt="Nafud" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Earth Science and Remote Sensing Unit, NASA Johnson Space Center · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ISS064-E-6310_-_View_of_Saudi_Arabia.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
